8
00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:40.260
How regulation shapes architecture, privacy by design under India's DPDP Act (and GDPR for comparison), payment-data localisation and card tokenisation, statutory retention, audit evidence, and data governance, turned into engineering mechanisms you can test.

9
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.889
Four runnable labs with real cryptography and hashing.

10
00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:47.095
L1: the right to erasure.

11
00:00:47.375 --> 00:00:52.751
A customer's data lived in 6 stores plus a legally retained GST invoice.

12
00:00:53.031 --> 00:01:15.517
Deleting from the primary left 5 copies readable (including the backup, with a sensitive shopping-basket inference); deleting everywhere we remembered left 2 (backup, warehouse); crypto-shredding (per-customer AES-256-GCM key destroyed) left 0, kept the invoice under a separate legal-retention key, and left the other customer intact.

13
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:19.107
L2: finding PII in logs.

14
00:01:19.387 --> 00:01:49.460
On 20,000 log lines with planted synthetic identifiers, format-only regexes had 1.9% precision for Aadhaar-format numbers and 0.9% for card numbers; checksums (Verhoeff, Luhn + issuer prefix) raised that to 16.4% and 97.8%; key-aware scanning reached 100% with no loss of recall, and redaction left 0 planted values and destroyed 0 order ids.

15
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.215
L3: residency as policy-as-code.

16
00:01:52.495 --> 00:01:54.948
10 data flows checked against 5 rules:

17
00:01:55.228 --> 00:02:05.639
5 blocked, including a Singapore DR replica holding payment data, card numbers in logs shipped to a vendor, and clickstream reused for ad targeting without consent.

18
00:02:05.919 --> 00:02:10.399
A full orders-DB replica may only go to Mumbai or Hyderabad.

19
00:02:11.749 --> 00:02:13.903
L4: tamper-evident audit log.

20
00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:19.645
103,500 hash-chained events (~25k per second).

21
00:02:19.925 --> 00:02:33.109
The chain alone caught edits, deletes and reorders but not a delete-and-recompute; external write-once anchors caught that, neither caught deletion of the 3,500 entries written since the last anchor.

