Part 1.

30
00:03:07.507 --> 00:03:09.427
Introduction and Why It Matters.

31
00:03:10.677 --> 00:03:14.837
A story: the audit that found what the architecture diagram didn't show.

32
00:03:15.117 --> 00:03:28.664
In its fourth year, the grocery platform prepared for three things at once: a payment-partner audit, its first formal privacy review under India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, and due diligence from an investor.

33
00:03:28.944 --> 00:03:31.077
The architecture diagrams looked clean.

34
00:03:31.357 --> 00:03:34.536
The auditors didn't look at diagrams; they looked at data.

35
00:03:34.816 --> 00:03:35.819
What they found:

36
00:03:37.169 --> 00:03:39.366
The DR region was in Singapore.

37
00:03:39.646 --> 00:03:56.030
Chosen years earlier for latency and price, it held a full replica of the orders database, including payment transaction data that the platform's payment-data policy (and RBI's 2018 direction) required to be stored only in India (L3).

38
00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.867
Card numbers were in the logs.

39
00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:09.881
A payment-adapter debug statement had logged request payloads for two weeks during an incident, and those logs had been shipped to a third-party observability vendor abroad (L2, L3).

40
00:04:11.231 --> 00:04:12.681
Delete my account didn't.

41
00:04:12.961 --> 00:04:15.649
The erasure feature deleted the customer row.

42
00:04:15.929 --> 00:04:26.148
Their name, phone, address and order history remained in the read model, the support search index, caches, nightly backups and the analytics warehouse, for years (L1).

43
00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:28.561
Nobody could prove who issued refunds.

44
00:04:28.841 --> 00:04:38.484
The refund audit table sat in the same database the operations team administered; several entries had been edited, and there was no way to show which (L4).

45
00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:41.818
Nobody knew where personal data was.

46
00:04:42.098 --> 00:04:50.034
There was no inventory of which stores held which categories of personal data, for what purpose, for how long, or who could access it.

47
00:04:50.314 --> 00:04:57.098
None of this was malice; each was an ordinary engineering choice made without the compliance constraint in view.

48
00:04:57.378 --> 00:05:12.354
The remediation took two quarters, moving the DR region, purging and re-shipping logs, retrofitting encryption for erasure, rebuilding the audit trail, and it would have cost a fraction of that if the constraints had been architecture requirements from the start.

49
00:05:13.704 --> 00:05:14.387
Quote:

50
00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:16.331
The thesis of this chapter.

51
00:05:16.611 --> 00:05:21.731
Compliance obligations are non-functional requirements with legal consequences.

52
00:05:22.011 --> 00:05:43.472
Treat them like latency or availability: identify them early, turn each into a concrete engineering mechanism (a key hierarchy, a residency policy, a retention rule, a tamper-evident log, a scanner), make those mechanisms testable and automated, and keep the evidence continuously rather than assembling it for an audit.

53
00:05:43.752 --> 00:05:58.152
The recurring architectural moves are few: know your data (inventory and classification), minimise it, encrypt it with keys you can destroy, control where it goes, retain it exactly as long as required, and prove what happened.

54
