101
00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:17.603
Part 2.

102
00:08:17.883 --> 00:08:19.761
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

103
00:08:21.011 --> 00:08:24.445
Intuition: compliance as non-functional requirements.

104
00:08:24.725 --> 00:08:30.528
Laws rarely say use AES-256 or put the DR region in Hyderabad.

105
00:08:30.808 --> 00:08:46.424
They state outcomes: protect personal data with reasonable safeguards; process it only for the purpose it was collected; erase it when that purpose is served; keep tax records for a period; store payment data in the country; report breaches promptly.

106
00:08:46.704 --> 00:08:49.136
The architect's job is the translation:

107
00:08:50.486 --> 00:08:51.830
Here is a quick table:

108
00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:55.523
Legal outcome, and Engineering requirement, and Mechanism.

109
00:08:55.803 --> 00:08:57.510
Erase when no longer needed.

110
00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.262
Engineering requirement:

111
00:08:59.542 --> 00:09:04.086
Every copy of a customer's data can be made unrecoverable within N days.

112
00:09:04.366 --> 00:09:05.305
Mechanism:

113
00:09:05.585 --> 00:09:07.163
Crypto-shredding (L1).

114
00:09:07.443 --> 00:09:09.342
Reasonable security safeguards.

115
00:09:09.622 --> 00:09:11.094
Engineering requirement:

116
00:09:11.374 --> 00:09:14.766
No plaintext personal data outside approved stores.

117
00:09:15.046 --> 00:09:15.985
Mechanism:

118
00:09:16.265 --> 00:09:19.678
Encryption, access control, PII scanning (L2).

119
00:09:19.958 --> 00:09:21.323
Store only in India.

120
00:09:21.603 --> 00:09:23.075
Engineering requirement:

121
00:09:23.355 --> 00:09:27.729
No replica, backup or export of class X outside Indian regions.

122
00:09:28.009 --> 00:09:28.947
Mechanism:

123
00:09:29.227 --> 00:09:31.681
Residency policy in CI (L3).

124
00:09:31.961 --> 00:09:33.497
Be able to show what happened.

125
00:09:33.777 --> 00:09:35.249
Engineering requirement:

126
00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:38.153
Tamper-evident record of privileged actions.

127
00:09:38.433 --> 00:09:39.371
Mechanism:

128
00:09:39.651 --> 00:09:42.297
Hash chain + external anchors (L4).

129
00:09:43.647 --> 00:09:45.631
Two principles run through everything:

130
00:09:45.911 --> 00:09:56.641
Privacy by design and by default, build the protection in (minimise, encrypt, restrict) rather than bolting it on; the default configuration should be the compliant one.

131
00:09:56.921 --> 00:10:04.516
Evidence, continuously, an obligation you can't demonstrate is, for an auditor or regulator, an obligation you didn't meet.

132
00:10:04.796 --> 00:10:11.687
Automate the evidence (scanner reports, policy results, verification runs) as part of normal operations.

133
00:10:13.037 --> 00:10:16.066
The regulatory landscape for an Indian platform.

134
00:10:16.346 --> 00:10:19.717
A summary for engineers, confirm specifics with counsel:

135
00:10:21.067 --> 00:10:26.101
Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act).

136
00:10:26.381 --> 00:10:28.557
India's general personal-data law.

137
00:10:28.837 --> 00:10:29.883
Key concepts:

138
00:10:30.163 --> 00:10:39.955
Data Principal (the individual), Data Fiduciary (who decides purpose and means, the platform), Data Processor (acts on the fiduciary's behalf, vendors).

139
00:10:40.235 --> 00:10:52.395
Processing on consent (free, specific, informed, unconditional, unambiguous, by clear affirmative action, withdrawable as easily as given) or for specified legitimate uses.

140
00:10:52.675 --> 00:11:09.955
Obligations include notice, purpose limitation, accuracy, reasonable security safeguards, breach notification to the Data Protection Board and affected individuals, erasure once the purpose is served (unless retention is required by law), grievance redressal,

141
00:11:10.235 --> 00:11:17.488
and additional duties for Significant Data Fiduciaries (for example, a Data Protection Officer, DPIAs, audits).

142
00:11:18.838 --> 00:11:28.033
Children's data needs verifiable parental consent and bars tracking or behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising directed at children.

143
00:11:28.313 --> 00:11:34.179
Cross-border transfer is permitted except to countries the government restricts by notification.

144
00:11:34.459 --> 00:11:42.929
Penalties are substantial (up to 250 crore rupees for a failure to take reasonable security safeguards, per the Act's schedule).

145
00:11:43.209 --> 00:11:59.614
The DPDP Rules, 2025 were notified in November 2025 with obligations phasing in over roughly 18 months, verify current commencement dates and the Rules' specifics, for example, breach-reporting timelines, with counsel.

146
00:12:00.964 --> 00:12:09.689
RBI payment-data localisation (2018 direction on storage of payment system data, with 2019 FAQs).

147
00:12:09.969 --> 00:12:25.372
Payment system operators must store the entire payment data in systems only in India; processing abroad is permitted if the data is deleted abroad and brought back within a short window (one business day or 24 hours per the FAQs).

148
00:12:25.652 --> 00:12:33.545
It applies to payment system operators and flows through to their partners via contracts, so merchants' architectures are shaped by it.

149
00:12:34.895 --> 00:12:39.930
RBI card-on-file tokenisation (effective October 2022).

150
00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:54.311
Merchants and payment aggregators (other than card networks and issuers) may not store customers' actual card data; they use network or issuer tokens (retaining only limited data such as the last four digits for reference).

151
00:12:54.591 --> 00:12:57.066
PCI DSS (v4.x).

152
00:12:57.346 --> 00:13:03.682
The card industry's security standard for any system that stores, processes or transmits card data.

153
00:13:03.962 --> 00:13:13.391
The main architectural lever is scope reduction: hosted payment pages, tokenisation, and keeping card data inside a small, isolated vault.

154
00:13:14.741 --> 00:13:16.491
GST record retention.

155
00:13:16.771 --> 00:13:30.467
Under the CGST Act (s.36), books and records, including invoices, must be kept for 72 months from the due date of the annual return for the year (longer if under appeal or investigation).

156
00:13:30.747 --> 00:13:33.691
CERT-In directions (April 2022).

157
00:13:33.971 --> 00:13:48.584
Report specified cyber incidents to CERT-In within 6 hours of noticing them; maintain ICT system logs for a rolling 180 days within Indian jurisdiction; synchronise clocks to specified NTP sources.

158
00:13:48.864 --> 00:13:51.595
Verify the current directions and applicability.

159
00:13:52.945 --> 00:14:14.150
GDPR (for EU residents' data, for example, an EU-based seller or customer), similar principles with notable differences: six lawful bases, 72-hour breach notification to the supervisory authority, transfer mechanisms (adequacy, standard contractual clauses), fines up to 4% of global turnover.

160
00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:26.355
Assurance frameworks, ISO or IEC 27001, SOC 2, aren't laws, but partners and enterprise customers require them; they're the governance scaffolding that evidence hangs on.

161
00:14:27.705 --> 00:14:47.097
The architectural consequences cluster into five themes: where data is stored and flows (residency), how long it's kept and how it's erased (retention or erasure), what is collected and for what (minimisation or purpose), how it's protected (security safeguards), and what can be proven (audit or evidence).

162
00:14:48.447 --> 00:14:51.285
Data inventory, classification and purpose.

163
00:14:51.565 --> 00:15:02.039
Every compliance mechanism depends on knowing what data you have, where it lives, why you have it, and how long you need it, the §1.1 platform knew none of these.

164
00:15:02.319 --> 00:15:06.287
Classify data into a small number of classes with handling rules.

165
00:15:06.567 --> 00:15:28.413
For the grocery platform: public (catalog), internal (operational metrics), personal (name, phone, address, order history), sensitive or special (inferences from baskets, health, pregnancy, religion; children's data), payment (transaction data subject to localisation), card (PAN, never outside the vault), legal-record (GST invoices).

166
00:15:29.763 --> 00:15:42.584
Inventory (a data catalog or record of processing): for each store, which classes it holds, owner, purpose(s), legal basis, retention, location, who can access it, which processors receive it.

167
00:15:42.864 --> 00:15:55.152
Tag at the source: schema annotations (@Personal, @Sensitive), column tags in the catalog, and purpose tags on data flows (L3) so tooling can enforce rules automatically.

168
00:15:55.432 --> 00:16:03.261
Discover what escaped: scan logs, exports, object storage and warehouses for identifiers that shouldn't be there (L2).

169
00:16:03.541 --> 00:16:07.616
Discovery is how you find the gaps between the inventory and reality.

170
00:16:08.966 --> 00:16:16.305
Purpose limitation and consent: record why each piece of personal data is processed, and check new uses against it.

171
00:16:16.585 --> 00:16:29.086
L3's clickstream flow (F4) reused personal data for ad targeting, a purpose the customer never agreed to, and was blocked; the aggregated, anonymised version (F5) passed.

172
00:16:29.366 --> 00:16:38.241
Consent should be recorded as data (who, what purpose, when, which notice version, withdrawn when) and checked at use, not just collected at sign-up.

173
00:16:39.591 --> 00:16:42.449
Minimisation, pseudonymisation and encryption.

174
00:16:42.729 --> 00:16:45.844
The cheapest data to protect is data you don't have.

175
00:16:46.124 --> 00:16:50.199
Minimise collection: don't ask for a date of birth to deliver groceries.

176
00:16:50.479 --> 00:17:00.783
Minimise propagation: pass IDs, not personal data, in events and to downstream systems; give each context only the fields it needs (Ch 33:

177
00:17:01.063 --> 00:17:05.009
Fulfilment gets a DeliveryLocation, not the customer profile).

178
00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:16.087
Pseudonymise for analytics: replace direct identifiers with keyed tokens (HMAC with a secret key), so analysts work without names and phone numbers.

179
00:17:16.367 --> 00:17:31.535
Pseudonymised data is still personal data under most laws (re-identification is possible with the key); anonymised aggregate data generally is not, but true anonymisation is harder than it looks (small groups, rare combinations).

180
00:17:31.815 --> 00:17:47.026
Encrypt at rest and in transit as a baseline (Ch 23); field-level encryption with per-subject or per-tenant keys for personal fields enables crypto-shredding (L1) and limits who can read them even with database access.

181
00:17:48.376 --> 00:17:55.672
Structured logging with allow-lists: log known-safe fields by name; never log whole request or response bodies.

182
00:17:55.952 --> 00:17:58.597
This is the upstream fix for L2's findings.

183
00:17:59.947 --> 00:18:03.382
Payment data: tokenisation and the PCI boundary.

184
00:18:03.662 --> 00:18:09.870
Card data is the most regulated data most platforms touch, and the best strategy is to not touch it:

185
00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:18.619
Hosted payment pages or SDKs from the payment aggregator collect card details directly, so card numbers never reach your servers.

186
00:18:18.899 --> 00:18:27.411
Network or issuer tokens replace stored card numbers for repeat payments (as RBI's card-on-file rules require of merchants).

187
00:18:28.761 --> 00:18:39.769
If any component must handle card data, isolate it in a small vault (its own network segment, accounts, keys and admins) and keep everything else out of PCI scope.

188
00:18:40.049 --> 00:18:46.236
L3's rule R2 blocks any flow carrying raw card numbers; L2 finds them where they leaked.

189
00:18:46.516 --> 00:19:00.745
Payment transaction data (amounts, parties, timestamps, references, not just card numbers) is what localisation rules address: store it in India, and design DR, analytics and vendor flows accordingly (L3).

190
00:19:01.025 --> 00:19:06.423
Foreign legs of processing (a card network abroad) are acceptable only when transient.

191
00:19:07.773 --> 00:19:10.354
Data residency and cross-border transfer.

192
00:19:10.634 --> 00:19:13.983
Residency constraints turn into concrete architecture:

193
00:19:14.263 --> 00:19:29.709
DR and replication: a full replica of a store containing localised data must stay in-country, for the grocery platform, a Mumbai primary with a Hyderabad DR region rather than Singapore (L3: the only allowed regions).

194
00:19:29.989 --> 00:19:36.410
This also matters for cells (Ch 34) and multi-region designs (Ch 27).

195
00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:41.810
Backups and snapshots are copies, they follow the same residency rules as the primary.

196
00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:49.496
Vendors (processors): observability, support desks, email or SMS, analytics, fraud tools.

197
00:19:49.776 --> 00:19:53.936
Each is a data flow with a destination, data classes and purpose.

198
00:19:54.216 --> 00:20:00.744
Send them the minimum (pseudonymised IDs, no card data), under a data-processing agreement, encrypted.

199
00:20:01.024 --> 00:20:09.621
Regional isolation for tenants or markets: per-region clusters or cells for data that must stay in a jurisdiction (Ch 30).

200
00:20:09.901 --> 00:20:16.771
Policy-as-code: encode the rules and evaluate every flow in the architecture manifest in CI (L3).

201
00:20:17.051 --> 00:20:23.557
Architects and reviewers then argue about the rules once, and every design is checked against them automatically.

202
00:20:24.907 --> 00:20:26.998
Retention and the right to erasure.

203
00:20:27.278 --> 00:20:35.299
Retention cuts both ways: keep data no longer than the purpose requires, and keep some records at least as long as the law requires.

204
00:20:35.579 --> 00:20:59.835
Retention schedule: per data class, debug logs 14 days; application logs 30 to 90 days hot then deleted (or longer only where required, for example, security logs per CERT-In's 180-day direction); order history for the customer relationship plus a defined period; GST invoices 72 plus months; marketing data until consent is withdrawn.

205
00:21:00.115 --> 00:21:06.046
Enforce with TTLs, lifecycle rules (Ch 35) and scheduled deletion jobs.

206
00:21:07.396 --> 00:21:20.687
Erasure is hard because data spreads (L1): the primary, CQRS read models, search indexes, caches, message logs (Kafka retention, compacted topics), backups, warehouses, vendor copies.

207
00:21:20.967 --> 00:21:28.071
Deleting from the primary left 5 readable copies; chasing copies still missed the immutable backup and the warehouse.

208
00:21:29.421 --> 00:21:40.877
Crypto-shredding makes erasure a single, provable action: encrypt personal fields with a per-customer key everywhere they're written (including backups and exports), and destroy the key.

209
00:21:41.157 --> 00:21:41.989
L1:

210
00:21:42.269 --> 00:21:46.087
0 readable copies afterwards, other customers untouched.

211
00:21:46.367 --> 00:22:02.773
Pre-conditions: personal data is never written in plaintext anywhere (logs, exports, caches), keys live in a KMS with deletion controls, and derived stores (search, warehouse) index encrypted fields or pseudonymous IDs rather than plaintext.

212
00:22:04.123 --> 00:22:21.147
Legal retention alongside erasure: data the law requires you to keep (GST invoices) is encrypted under a separate legal-retention key with only the fields the law needs; it's retained, access-restricted, and deleted when the retention period ends.

213
00:22:21.427 --> 00:22:31.624
Erasure log: record that an erasure happened (request, subject ID, keys destroyed, what was retained and why, when), without the personal data, as evidence.

214
00:22:31.904 --> 00:22:40.523
Backups: rotate within a bounded period, so even without crypto-shredding the data ages out; with crypto-shredding it's unreadable immediately.

215
00:22:41.873 --> 00:22:52.390
Legal holds (litigation, investigations) override deletion for specific subjects; the retention system must support suspending deletion with an audit trail.

216
00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:55.361
Audit trails and evidence.

217
00:22:55.641 --> 00:23:11.769
Accountability obligations and forensics need a reliable record of who did what to which object, when, especially privileged actions: viewing personal data in support tools, issuing refunds, changing roles, exporting data, changing configuration.

218
00:23:12.049 --> 00:23:14.695
What makes an audit trail trustworthy (L4):

219
00:23:14.975 --> 00:23:27.604
Completeness: emitted from the application for every sensitive action, including by service accounts; include actor, action, target, time (synchronised clocks), source, and a correlation ID.

220
00:23:28.954 --> 00:23:40.901
Tamper evidence: a hash chain detects edits, deletions and reorders, but an insider who controls the whole store can delete an entry and recompute every later hash (L4 case 4).

221
00:23:41.181 --> 00:23:54.066
External anchoring: periodically write the chain head to a write-once store outside the insider's control (object storage with Object Lock or WORM in a separate account, or a public timestamping service).

222
00:23:54.346 --> 00:24:05.119
That defeats rewrites; the remaining gap is entries since the last anchor (L4 case 5), shrink it by anchoring frequently or streaming each entry to the external store.

223
00:24:06.469 --> 00:24:12.400
Separation of duties: the people who administer the application can't administer the audit store.

224
00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:18.739
Retention and access: keep audit logs as long as required; restrict and audit access to them.

225
00:24:19.019 --> 00:24:27.360
Verification as a job: verify the chain and anchors continuously and alert on failure, evidence you never check isn't evidence.

226
00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:42.193
Beyond audit logs, compliance evidence includes access-review records, policy-check results (L3), scanner reports (L2), key-destruction records (L1), change approvals and DR test results.

227
00:24:42.473 --> 00:24:46.121
Automate collection so an audit is a report, not a project.

228
00:24:47.471 --> 00:24:48.559
Breach readiness.

229
00:24:48.839 --> 00:24:52.465
Breach notification obligations have short clocks (CERT-In:

230
00:24:52.745 --> 00:25:03.583
6 hours for reportable incidents; DPDP: prompt notification to the Board and affected individuals, with details specified in the Rules; GDPR:

231
00:25:03.863 --> 00:25:05.121
72 hours).

232
00:25:05.401 --> 00:25:07.385
You can't meet them without preparation:

233
00:25:07.665 --> 00:25:12.529
Know which data is where (inventory) so you can scope impact in hours, not weeks.

234
00:25:12.809 --> 00:25:18.975
Keep logs and audit trails sufficient to reconstruct access (and retained per CERT-In where applicable).

235
00:25:20.325 --> 00:25:26.341
A runbook with legal, security, communications and engineering roles, and pre-drafted notices.

236
00:25:26.621 --> 00:25:34.834
Encryption and minimisation reduce impact: a stolen backup of crypto-shredded or field-encrypted data exposes far less.

237
00:25:35.114 --> 00:25:40.063
Practise it (tabletop exercises), like DR drills (Ch 27).

238
00:25:41.413 --> 00:25:42.971
Data governance at scale.

239
00:25:43.251 --> 00:25:49.480
Governance is how an organisation keeps these mechanisms working as teams and systems multiply:

240
00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:55.776
Ownership: every data store has an owning team and a data steward; every class has handling rules.

241
00:25:56.056 --> 00:26:07.235
Catalog + lineage: where data came from and where it flows (lineage from pipelines or CDC), so an erasure or a residency rule can be traced through derived datasets.

242
00:26:08.585 --> 00:26:21.342
Policy-as-code everywhere it can be: residency and transfer rules (L3), required encryption and tags at provisioning, retention defaults, allowed log fields, checked in CI and at deploy.

243
00:26:21.622 --> 00:26:30.475
Access governance: least privilege, just-in-time elevated access for production data with approval and audit, periodic access reviews.

244
00:26:30.755 --> 00:26:39.417
Privacy reviews or DPIAs for new features that process personal data in new ways, part of the design-doc process (Ch 32).

245
00:26:39.697 --> 00:26:51.515
Metrics: % of stores classified, % of flows passing policy, PII findings in logs per week (L2), erasure SLA, audit-verification status (L4).

246
00:26:52.865 --> 00:27:04.876
Proportionality: as with cost (Ch 35), concentrate effort where the risk is, payment data, children's data, sensitive inferences, rather than equal ceremony for every table.

