385
00:41:32.361 --> 00:41:33.215
Reading it:

386
00:41:33.495 --> 00:41:36.588
Format-only is useless for numeric identifiers:

387
00:41:36.868 --> 00:41:45.444
1.9% precision for Aadhaar-format numbers and 0.9% for cards, timestamps and order ids dominate.

388
00:41:45.724 --> 00:41:47.367
Alerts like that get ignored.

389
00:41:47.647 --> 00:41:50.015
Checksums help a lot but not completely:

390
00:41:50.295 --> 00:42:04.631
Luhn + issuer prefix makes the card detector 97.8% precise; Verhoeff only reaches 16.4%, because ~10% of random 12-digit order ids pass a checksum by chance.

391
00:42:04.911 --> 00:42:08.324
Redacting those would destroy the order ids engineers need.

392
00:42:09.674 --> 00:42:30.069
Structure closes the gap: excluding values of known non-PII keys (order=, ts=, trace=) reaches 100% precision with 100% recall on every detector, and the redaction pass removes every planted value while destroying 0 order ids or timestamps.

393
00:42:30.349 --> 00:42:42.231
That 100% is partly by construction, the synthetic logs use known keys, which is exactly the argument for structured logging: when logs have structure, scanning becomes precise.

394
00:42:42.511 --> 00:42:50.277
The upstream fix is still the one that matters: log allow-listed fields only, so personal data isn't written in the first place.

395
00:42:51.627 --> 00:42:55.147
L3, Residency and transfer rules as policy-as-code.

396
00:42:55.427 --> 00:42:56.365
Requirement:

397
00:42:56.645 --> 00:43:05.669
Before any design ships, check every data flow, replicas, exports, vendors, against our residency, card-data, purpose and retention rules.

398
00:43:05.949 --> 00:43:15.421
Ten flows from the architecture manifest, each with data classes, destination region, purpose and safeguards, are evaluated against five rules.

399
00:43:15.701 --> 00:43:22.741
The rules are an illustrative engineering encoding of obligations the platform has identified, not legal advice.

400
00:43:24.091 --> 00:43:27.291
Here is the Java code, ResidencyL3.java.

401
00:43:27.571 --> 00:43:31.625
It runs to 150 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

402
00:43:31.905 --> 00:43:39.542
ResidencyL3.java, run with: java ResidencyL3.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

403
00:43:39.822 --> 00:43:43.257
DATA RESIDENCY AND TRANSFER RULES AS POLICY-AS-CODE.

404
00:43:43.537 --> 00:43:54.758
Architects draw data flows, DR replicas, analytics exports, log. shipping, vendor integrations, and each one moves some CLASSES of data to some PLACE for some PURPOSE.

405
00:43:55.038 --> 00:43:55.998
Whether that's.

406
00:43:58.848 --> 00:43:59.765
On screen:

407
00:44:00.045 --> 00:44:06.979
Evaluating 10 data flows from the architecture manifest against 5 residency or transfer. rules.

408
00:44:07.259 --> 00:44:12.549
F1 orders DB, then DR replica (Hyderabad), then INHYDERABAD PASS.

409
00:44:12.829 --> 00:44:17.437
F2 orders DB, then DR replica (Singapore), then SG BLOCK.

410
00:44:17.717 --> 00:44:22.347
R1 payment-data localisation payment data would be STORED in SG.

411
00:44:22.627 --> 00:44:26.957
F3 payment auth, then card network (abroad leg), then US PASS.

412
00:44:27.237 --> 00:44:31.291
F4 clickstream, then US marketing SaaS, then US BLOCK.

413
00:44:31.571 --> 00:44:38.675
R3 purpose limitation 'behavioural' data used for 'ad-targeting' without a. matching purpose or consent.

414
00:44:38.955 --> 00:44:45.888
R3 purpose limitation 'personal' data used for 'ad-targeting' without a. matching purpose or consent.

415
00:44:46.168 --> 00:44:51.096
F5 clickstream, then US marketing SaaS (aggregated), then US PASS.

416
00:44:51.376 --> 00:44:52.891
The rest is in the chapter.

417
00:44:55.741 --> 00:44:56.594
Reading it:

418
00:44:56.874 --> 00:45:02.677
F2, a DR replica in Singapore, is blocked: it would store payment data outside India.

419
00:45:02.957 --> 00:45:12.343
F1, the Hyderabad replica with the same data, passes; the engine computes that a full orders replica may only go to Mumbai or Hyderabad.

420
00:45:12.623 --> 00:45:19.429
F3, the card network's foreign leg, passes only because it is processing-only with deletion abroad within a day.

421
00:45:19.709 --> 00:45:28.391
F8, a fraud vendor that would store payment data in the US, is blocked; sending it tokenised or pseudonymised signals would change that.

422
00:45:29.741 --> 00:45:36.632
F6, logs to an EU observability vendor, is blocked because they carry card numbers (what L2 finds).

423
00:45:36.912 --> 00:45:48.432
F4 reuses personal and behavioural data for ad targeting without a matching purpose, blocked; F5, the aggregated or anonymised version of the same export, passes.

424
00:45:48.712 --> 00:45:55.347
F9 would archive invoices with a 36-month retention, shorter than the statutory period, blocked.

425
00:45:55.627 --> 00:46:06.016
In production this runs as OPA or Rego (or similar) over the service manifests and Terraform plans in CI, with data classes drawn from the catalog.

426
00:46:07.366 --> 00:46:09.627
L4, A tamper-evident audit log.

427
00:46:09.907 --> 00:46:10.846
Requirement:

428
00:46:11.126 --> 00:46:14.070
Refund records were edited and we can't tell which.

429
00:46:14.350 --> 00:46:19.001
Build an audit trail an insider with database access can't silently rewrite.

430
00:46:19.281 --> 00:46:30.929
103,500 audit events are appended to a SHA-256 hash chain; every 10,000th chain head is anchored to an external write-once store.

431
00:46:31.209 --> 00:46:37.886
Then an insider tries to hide a specific refund five ways, and verification runs with and without the anchors.

432
00:46:39.236 --> 00:46:42.393
Here is the Java code, AuditLogL4.java.

433
00:46:42.673 --> 00:46:47.047
It runs to 164 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

434
00:46:47.327 --> 00:46:54.921
AuditLogL4.java, run with: java AuditLogL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

435
00:46:55.201 --> 00:46:56.972
A TAMPER-EVIDENT AUDIT LOG.

436
00:46:57.252 --> 00:47:06.511
Regulators, auditors and incident responders need to trust who did what to which record. when, refunds issued, PII viewed, roles granted.

437
00:47:06.791 --> 00:47:10.993
An audit table in the same database the admins control proves nothing:

438
00:47:13.843 --> 00:47:14.761
On screen:

439
00:47:15.041 --> 00:47:28.545
Appended 103,500 audit events in 4091 ms (25,297 per second); full verification in 2166 ms.

440
00:47:28.825 --> 00:47:32.387
10. external anchors (last at seq 100,000).

441
00:47:32.667 --> 00:47:33.627
Clean log:

442
00:47:33.907 --> 00:47:34.846
VERIFIED.

443
00:47:35.126 --> 00:47:48.417
Insider cover-up of entry 42,008 (ISSUEREFUND by ops.kiran, amountPaise=40476): manipulation hash chain alone chain + external anchors.

444
00:47:48.697 --> 00:47:56.334
1. change the actor on the entry DETECTED DETECTED, entry 42008 content does not. match its hash.

445
00:47:56.614 --> 00:48:03.035
2. delete the entry DETECTED DETECTED, sequence gap or reorder at position 42008.

446
00:48:03.315 --> 00:48:05.363
(found seq 42009).

447
00:48:05.643 --> 00:48:16.161
3. swap it with the next entry (reorder) DETECTED DETECTED, sequence gap or reorder. at position 42008 (found seq 42009).

448
00:48:16.441 --> 00:48:17.955
The rest is in the chapter.

449
00:48:20.805 --> 00:48:21.659
Reading it:

450
00:48:21.939 --> 00:48:27.443
Edits, deletions and reorders (1 to 3) break the chain and are caught by the chain alone.

451
00:48:27.723 --> 00:48:34.037
Delete-and-recompute (4) produces an internally consistent chain, invisible to the chain alone.

452
00:48:34.317 --> 00:48:43.277
The external anchor at sequence 50,000 exposes it: the recomputed chain no longer matches what was written outside the insider's reach.

453
00:48:44.627 --> 00:48:51.817
Deleting entries written since the last anchor (5) is caught by neither, the unanchored window is the residual risk.

454
00:48:52.097 --> 00:49:01.590
Anchor frequently (for example, every minute) or stream every entry to the external store, and keep that store in a separate account with separate administrators.

455
00:49:01.870 --> 00:49:13.326
Throughput here is ~25k entries per second single-threaded (with simple hex formatting); shard chains per service or partition and anchor a combined root for higher volumes.

456
00:49:14.676 --> 00:49:15.615
Part 6.

457
00:49:15.895 --> 00:49:17.175
Production Concerns.

458
00:49:18.425 --> 00:49:19.577
Failure modes.

459
00:49:19.857 --> 00:49:21.201
Here is a quick table:

460
00:49:21.481 --> 00:49:23.763
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

461
00:49:24.043 --> 00:49:25.579
Erasure misses copies.

462
00:49:25.859 --> 00:49:26.670
Symptom:

463
00:49:26.950 --> 00:49:30.641
Data survives in read models, backups, warehouse (L1).

464
00:49:30.921 --> 00:49:31.923
Mitigation:

465
00:49:32.203 --> 00:49:35.190
Crypto-shredding; no plaintext PII anywhere.

466
00:49:35.470 --> 00:49:38.009
Plaintext PII leaks around encryption.

467
00:49:38.289 --> 00:49:39.099
Symptom:

468
00:49:39.379 --> 00:49:41.918
Logs, exports, caches written in clear.

469
00:49:42.198 --> 00:49:43.201
Mitigation:

470
00:49:43.481 --> 00:49:47.171
Structured logging allow-lists; scanners on every sink (L2).

471
00:49:47.451 --> 00:49:49.478
Key loss or premature destruction.

472
00:49:49.758 --> 00:49:50.569
Symptom:

473
00:49:50.849 --> 00:49:53.430
Unrecoverable data for active customers.

474
00:49:53.710 --> 00:49:54.713
Mitigation:

475
00:49:54.993 --> 00:50:01.009
KMS with deletion windows, two-person approval for bulk destruction, backups of wrapped keys.

476
00:50:01.289 --> 00:50:04.062
Residency violation via infrastructure.

477
00:50:04.342 --> 00:50:05.153
Symptom:

478
00:50:05.433 --> 00:50:08.035
DR, backups, vendors abroad (L3).

479
00:50:08.315 --> 00:50:09.318
Mitigation:

480
00:50:09.598 --> 00:50:12.691
Flows in a manifest; policy-as-code in CI.

481
00:50:12.971 --> 00:50:13.910
Purpose creep.

482
00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:15.001
Symptom:

483
00:50:15.281 --> 00:50:18.011
Data reused for new purposes without consent.

484
00:50:18.291 --> 00:50:19.294
Mitigation:

485
00:50:19.574 --> 00:50:23.051
Purpose tags on flows; privacy review in design docs.

486
00:50:24.401 --> 00:50:25.767
Editable audit trail.

487
00:50:26.047 --> 00:50:26.857
Symptom:

488
00:50:27.137 --> 00:50:29.356
Evidence can't be trusted (L4).

489
00:50:29.636 --> 00:50:30.639
Mitigation:

490
00:50:30.919 --> 00:50:34.417
Hash chain + external anchors + separation of duties.

491
00:50:34.697 --> 00:50:35.764
Over-retention.

492
00:50:36.044 --> 00:50:36.855
Symptom:

493
00:50:37.135 --> 00:50:38.927
Old data kept just in case.

494
00:50:39.207 --> 00:50:40.209
Mitigation:

495
00:50:40.489 --> 00:50:44.265
Retention schedule enforced by TTL or lifecycle.

496
00:50:44.545 --> 00:50:45.612
Under-retention.

497
00:50:45.892 --> 00:50:46.703
Symptom:

498
00:50:46.983 --> 00:50:48.817
Statutory records deleted.

499
00:50:49.097 --> 00:50:50.100
Mitigation:

500
00:50:50.380 --> 00:50:53.601
Legal-retention class, WORM storage, legal holds.

501
00:50:53.881 --> 00:50:55.268
Slow breach response.

502
00:50:55.548 --> 00:50:56.359
Symptom:

503
00:50:56.639 --> 00:50:58.495
Can't scope impact within hours.

504
00:50:58.775 --> 00:50:59.777
Mitigation:

505
00:51:00.057 --> 00:51:02.361
Inventory, logs, runbook, drills.

506
00:51:03.711 --> 00:51:04.799
Observability.

507
00:51:05.079 --> 00:51:06.978
Dashboards for compliance signals:

508
00:51:07.258 --> 00:51:19.610
PII findings per day by source (L2), policy-check failures (L3), erasure requests and SLA, audit-verification status and unanchored window (L4), key destructions.

509
00:51:19.890 --> 00:51:29.959
Access logs for personal data (support tool views, exports) feed the audit log and anomaly detection (an agent viewing hundreds of customer profiles).

510
00:51:30.239 --> 00:51:39.285
Be careful that observability itself doesn't become a PII store, the logs, traces and dashboards are data sinks too (Ch 24).

511
00:51:40.635 --> 00:51:41.573
Security.

512
00:51:41.853 --> 00:51:50.195
Compliance and security overlap but aren't the same: you can be compliant and breached, or secure and non-compliant (keeping data too long).

513
00:51:50.475 --> 00:51:52.757
Design for both (Ch 23).

514
00:51:53.037 --> 00:52:01.891
KMS as crown jewels: key-destruction and key-use permissions are the most sensitive in the system; separate duties and audit every use.

515
00:52:02.171 --> 00:52:08.507
Separate accounts for audit anchors, backups and the payment vault limit what one compromised admin can alter.

516
00:52:08.787 --> 00:52:17.192
Treat vendors as part of the attack surface: minimise what they receive, contract for security and breach notification, review regularly.

517
00:52:18.542 --> 00:52:19.865
Performance trade-offs.

518
00:52:20.145 --> 00:52:33.926
Field-level encryption adds CPU and latency per field and prevents database-side search on encrypted columns, use blind indexes (HMAC tokens) for equality lookups and pseudonymous IDs in search.

519
00:52:34.206 --> 00:52:48.606
Hash-chaining serialises audit writes per chain; shard chains by partition (per service or per time window) and anchor each (L4: ~25k entries per second single-threaded with naive hex formatting).

520
00:52:48.886 --> 00:52:59.595
Scanning every log line costs CPU; scan at the pipeline with sampling for high-volume, low-risk streams and fully for high-risk sinks (exports, vendor-bound logs).

521
00:53:00.945 --> 00:53:01.671
Cost.

522
00:53:01.951 --> 00:53:10.036
An in-country DR region may cost more than a cheaper foreign one; residency is a constraint, not an option to optimise away.

523
00:53:10.316 --> 00:53:18.743
Encryption and KMS calls add cost at scale, cache data keys (envelope encryption) rather than calling the KMS per field.

524
00:53:19.023 --> 00:53:31.716
The cost of non-compliance, penalties, remediation projects (§1.1's two quarters), lost partners, dwarfs the mechanisms, but apply them proportionately to risk (Ch 35).

525
00:53:33.066 --> 00:53:34.005
Part 7.

526
00:53:34.285 --> 00:53:35.991
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

527
00:53:37.241 --> 00:53:38.159
Watch out:

528
00:53:38.439 --> 00:53:39.079
1.

529
00:53:39.359 --> 00:53:41.471
Compliance as a pre-audit project.

530
00:53:41.751 --> 00:53:44.844
Evidence assembled once a year is stale and incomplete.

531
00:53:45.124 --> 00:53:48.324
Build mechanisms and collect evidence continuously.

532
00:53:49.674 --> 00:53:50.591
Watch out:

533
00:53:50.871 --> 00:53:51.575
2.

534
00:53:51.855 --> 00:53:54.223
Erasure = DELETE FROM customers.

535
00:53:54.503 --> 00:53:59.623
Leaves read models, indexes, caches, backups and warehouses readable (L1).

536
00:53:59.903 --> 00:54:02.122
Design erasure across every copy.

537
00:54:03.472 --> 00:54:04.389
Watch out:

538
00:54:04.669 --> 00:54:05.416
3.

539
00:54:05.696 --> 00:54:08.021
Crypto-shredding with plaintext side-channels.

540
00:54:08.301 --> 00:54:14.872
Encrypting the database while logs, exports and caches hold plaintext makes the key destruction meaningless.

541
00:54:15.152 --> 00:54:17.072
No plaintext PII anywhere.

542
00:54:18.422 --> 00:54:19.339
Watch out:

543
00:54:19.619 --> 00:54:20.323
4.

544
00:54:20.603 --> 00:54:22.075
Logging request bodies.

545
00:54:22.355 --> 00:54:27.582
The most common way card numbers and personal data leak (§1.1, L2).

546
00:54:27.862 --> 00:54:29.761
Log allow-listed fields only.

547
00:54:31.111 --> 00:54:32.028
Watch out:

548
00:54:32.308 --> 00:54:33.076
5.

549
00:54:33.356 --> 00:54:35.255
Format-only PII scanning.

550
00:54:35.535 --> 00:54:37.711
Alerts drown in look-alikes (L2:

551
00:54:37.991 --> 00:54:40.849
1.9% precision) and get ignored.

552
00:54:41.129 --> 00:54:43.327
Validate candidates and use structure.

553
00:54:44.677 --> 00:54:45.594
Watch out:

554
00:54:45.874 --> 00:54:46.621
6.

555
00:54:46.901 --> 00:54:50.165
DR region chosen for price and latency alone.

556
00:54:50.445 --> 00:54:54.733
Replicas and backups are copies subject to residency rules (L3).

557
00:54:55.013 --> 00:54:56.805
Check the rules before the region.

558
00:54:58.155 --> 00:54:59.072
Watch out:

559
00:54:59.352 --> 00:55:00.099
7.

560
00:55:00.379 --> 00:55:02.000
Vendors as an afterthought.

561
00:55:02.280 --> 00:55:07.635
Every observability, support and analytics tool is a cross-border data flow with a purpose.

562
00:55:07.915 --> 00:55:09.813
Minimise, contract, record.

563
00:55:11.163 --> 00:55:12.081
Watch out:

564
00:55:12.361 --> 00:55:13.043
8.

565
00:55:13.323 --> 00:55:15.286
Consent collected but never checked.

566
00:55:15.566 --> 00:55:19.022
A checkbox at sign-up that no data flow ever consults.

567
00:55:19.302 --> 00:55:23.633
Record consent as data and check purpose at use (L3 R3).

568
00:55:24.983 --> 00:55:25.900
Watch out:

569
00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:26.927
9.

570
00:55:27.207 --> 00:55:29.745
Audit table in the application database.

571
00:55:30.025 --> 00:55:33.524
Anyone who administers it can edit it (§1.1).

572
00:55:33.804 --> 00:55:36.449
Hash-chain it and anchor externally (L4).

573
00:55:37.799 --> 00:55:38.717
Watch out:

574
00:55:38.997 --> 00:55:39.701
10.

575
00:55:39.981 --> 00:55:41.901
Hash chain without external anchors.

576
00:55:42.181 --> 00:55:45.573
An insider can delete and recompute (L4 case 4).

577
00:55:45.853 --> 00:55:47.559
Anchor to a store they can't write.

578
00:55:48.909 --> 00:55:49.827
Watch out:

579
00:55:50.107 --> 00:55:50.939
11.

580
00:55:51.219 --> 00:55:52.755
Keeping everything forever.

581
00:55:53.035 --> 00:55:57.301
Over-retention is itself a violation and increases breach impact.

582
00:55:57.581 --> 00:55:59.309
Retention schedule per class.

583
00:56:00.659 --> 00:56:01.577
Watch out:

584
00:56:01.857 --> 00:56:02.646
12.

585
00:56:02.926 --> 00:56:05.230
Storing card numbers 'just for refunds'.

586
00:56:05.510 --> 00:56:12.593
Tokens do that job; storing card data brings PCI scope and violates card-on-file rules for merchants.

587
00:56:13.943 --> 00:56:14.817
Part 8.

588
00:56:15.097 --> 00:56:16.441
Interview Q and A.

589
00:56:17.691 --> 00:56:18.523
Beginner.

590
00:56:18.803 --> 00:56:19.827
Question 1.

591
00:56:20.107 --> 00:56:23.115
How do regulations become architecture requirements?

592
00:56:23.395 --> 00:56:24.761
Thirty-second answer:

593
00:56:25.041 --> 00:56:37.115
Laws state outcomes, protect personal data, use it only for its purpose, erase it when no longer needed, keep tax records for a period, store payment data in-country, report breaches quickly.

594
00:56:37.395 --> 00:56:50.963
The architect translates each into a testable engineering requirement and a mechanism: crypto-shredding for erasure, policy-as-code for residency, a tamper-evident log for accountability, scanners for data leakage.

595
00:56:51.243 --> 00:56:52.843
Then automate the evidence.

596
00:56:54.193 --> 00:56:55.196
Question 2.

597
00:56:55.476 --> 00:56:58.015
What is data residency or localisation?

598
00:56:58.295 --> 00:56:59.660
Thirty-second answer:

599
00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:06.084
A requirement that certain data be stored (and sometimes processed) only within a country or region.

600
00:57:06.364 --> 00:57:20.273
For an Indian payments-related platform, RBI's 2018 direction requires payment system data to be stored only in India; transient processing abroad is allowed if the data is deleted there quickly.

601
00:57:20.553 --> 00:57:26.825
Architecturally it constrains where replicas, backups, DR regions, analytics and vendors can be.

602
00:57:28.175 --> 00:57:29.221
Question 3.

603
00:57:29.501 --> 00:57:30.866
What is crypto-shredding?

604
00:57:31.146 --> 00:57:32.511
Thirty-second answer:

605
00:57:32.791 --> 00:57:40.749
Encrypting a subject's personal data with a key specific to them, in every place it's written, and erasing the data by destroying the key.

606
00:57:41.029 --> 00:57:49.093
Every copy, primary, replicas, backups, exports, becomes unreadable at once, without having to find and delete each copy.

607
00:57:49.373 --> 00:57:52.509
Other subjects' data, under other keys, is unaffected.

608
00:57:53.859 --> 00:57:54.925
Question 4.

609
00:57:55.205 --> 00:57:56.827
Why tokenise card data?

610
00:57:57.107 --> 00:57:58.472
Thirty-second answer:

611
00:57:58.752 --> 00:58:03.232
So your systems never store, and ideally never see, actual card numbers.

612
00:58:03.512 --> 00:58:09.677
Hosted payment pages collect them and network or issuer tokens stand in for them on repeat payments.

613
00:58:09.957 --> 00:58:21.499
That keeps most of the platform out of PCI DSS scope, removes the highest-value data from breach impact, and meets RBI's rule that merchants don't store card-on-file data.

614
00:58:22.849 --> 00:58:23.851
Intermediate.

615
00:58:24.131 --> 00:58:25.241
Question 5.

616
00:58:25.521 --> 00:58:31.878
Design the right-to-erasure feature for a platform with CQRS, search, Kafka, backups and a warehouse.

617
00:58:32.158 --> 00:58:33.523
Thirty-second answer:

618
00:58:33.803 --> 00:58:43.979
Encrypt personal fields at write time with a per-customer key from a KMS, in every store and message, and keep only pseudonymous IDs in search and the warehouse.

619
00:58:44.259 --> 00:58:57.529
On a verified request: check legal holds and retention; destroy the customer key (all copies become unreadable, including backups); tidy up live stores (delete rows, index documents, cache entries,

620
00:58:57.809 --> 00:59:08.454
compact topics); keep legally required records under a separate retention key with minimal fields; write an erasure log without personal data; confirm to the customer.

621
00:59:09.804 --> 00:59:10.913
Question 6.

622
00:59:11.193 --> 00:59:13.732
How do you find personal data where it shouldn't be?

623
00:59:14.012 --> 00:59:15.377
Thirty-second answer:

624
00:59:15.657 --> 00:59:38.356
Scan the sinks, logs, exports, object storage, warehouses, vendor feeds, with detectors for the identifiers you care about (phones, emails, Aadhaar-format numbers, PAN, card numbers), validate candidates with each identifier's rules (Verhoeff, Luhn + issuer prefix, PAN structure) and use structure (field names or keys) to exclude known non-PII fields.

625
00:59:38.636 --> 00:59:42.391
Redact at the pipeline, alert the owning team, and fix the source.

626
00:59:43.741 --> 00:59:44.829
Question 7.

627
00:59:45.109 --> 00:59:48.159
What does purpose limitation mean for system design?

628
00:59:48.439 --> 00:59:49.805
Thirty-second answer:

629
00:59:50.085 --> 00:59:56.015
Personal data may be used only for the purposes it was collected for (consent or legitimate use).

630
00:59:56.295 --> 01:00:09.842
In design terms: record the purpose(s) for each data class, tag data flows with their purpose, check new uses against recorded purposes and consent, and require a privacy review when a new purpose appears.

631
01:00:10.122 --> 01:00:14.133
Aggregated or anonymised data can often be used more freely.

632
01:00:15.483 --> 01:00:16.507
Question 8.

633
01:00:16.787 --> 01:00:18.387
How long should you keep data?

634
01:00:18.667 --> 01:00:20.032
Thirty-second answer:

635
01:00:20.312 --> 01:00:25.880
Exactly as long as its purpose or a legal obligation requires, no longer, no shorter.

636
01:00:26.160 --> 01:00:45.147
Set a retention schedule per data class (debug logs days, app logs weeks to months, security logs per regulator direction, order history per relationship, GST invoices 72 plus months), enforce it with TTLs and lifecycle rules, support legal holds, and record deletions.

637
01:00:46.497 --> 01:00:47.563
Question 9.

638
01:00:47.843 --> 01:00:49.721
What makes an audit log trustworthy?

639
01:00:50.001 --> 01:00:51.366
Thirty-second answer:

640
01:00:51.646 --> 01:01:10.334
Completeness (every privileged action, with actor, action, target, time and source), tamper evidence (hash chain), external anchoring (chain heads written to a write-once store outside the operators' control), separation of duties, appropriate retention, and continuous verification with alerts.

641
01:01:11.684 --> 01:01:12.452
Senior.

642
01:01:12.732 --> 01:01:13.777
Question 10.

643
01:01:14.057 --> 01:01:18.089
Design DR for a platform whose payment data must stay in India.

644
01:01:18.369 --> 01:01:19.735
Thirty-second answer:

645
01:01:20.015 --> 01:01:36.527
Primary in one Indian region, DR in another Indian region, with replication, backups and snapshots staying in-country; anything leaving the country is transient processing (deleted promptly), anonymised or aggregated, or stripped of payment data.

646
01:01:36.807 --> 01:01:42.780
Encode this as policy-as-code over the architecture's data flows so a foreign replica fails CI.

647
01:01:44.130 --> 01:01:45.261
Question 11.

648
01:01:45.541 --> 01:01:49.103
How do you make search and analytics work with encrypted personal data?

649
01:01:49.383 --> 01:01:50.749
Thirty-second answer:

650
01:01:51.029 --> 01:01:52.586
Don't index plaintext.

651
01:01:52.866 --> 01:02:09.293
Use pseudonymous tokens (keyed HMAC of identifiers) as join keys and for equality search (blind indexes); keep analytics on pseudonymised or aggregated data; decrypt only in the service that needs the value, for the user who's allowed to see it.

652
01:02:09.573 --> 01:02:20.879
For free-text search over personal data (support tools), use a separate, access-controlled, audited index whose entries are encrypted under the subject key so crypto-shredding covers it.

653
01:02:22.229 --> 01:02:23.317
Question 12.

654
01:02:23.597 --> 01:02:28.333
How do you encode compliance rules as code without turning engineers into lawyers?

655
01:02:28.613 --> 01:02:29.979
Thirty-second answer:

656
01:02:30.259 --> 01:02:48.669
Legal and compliance define the rules in plain language; a small platform team encodes them as policies over data classes, regions, purposes and safeguards; engineers declare their data flows in a manifest (or it's derived from infrastructure code) and CI evaluates them.

657
01:02:48.949 --> 01:02:54.731
Engineers see concrete failures (payment data would be stored in SG), not legal text.

658
01:02:56.081 --> 01:02:57.361
Question 13.

659
01:02:57.641 --> 01:03:01.395
What are the architectural implications of processing children's data?

660
01:03:01.675 --> 01:03:03.041
Thirty-second answer:

661
01:03:03.321 --> 01:03:19.257
Under the DPDP Act, processing a child's personal data needs verifiable parental consent, and tracking, behavioural monitoring and targeted advertising directed at children are not allowed (subject to exemptions the Rules may define).

662
01:03:19.537 --> 01:03:34.406
Architecturally: know whether a user is a child (age assurance at onboarding), flag their data as a special class, exclude it from personalisation, ad and profiling pipelines by policy, and apply stricter retention and access.

663
01:03:35.756 --> 01:03:36.951
Question 14.

664
01:03:37.231 --> 01:03:40.025
How would you prepare to notify a breach within hours?

665
01:03:40.305 --> 01:03:41.671
Thirty-second answer:

666
01:03:41.951 --> 01:04:01.023
Know what data is where (inventory), keep sufficient access logs and audit trails (retained and in the required jurisdiction), have a runbook with legal or security or comms or engineering roles and pre-drafted notices, practise it, and reduce impact in advance with minimisation and encryption.

667
01:04:01.303 --> 01:04:04.033
Detection and scoping speed are the bottlenecks.

668
01:04:05.383 --> 01:04:06.279
Architect.

669
01:04:06.559 --> 01:04:07.754
Question 15.

670
01:04:08.034 --> 01:04:12.450
Design a compliance architecture for a new Indian quick-commerce platform.

671
01:04:12.730 --> 01:04:14.095
Thirty-second answer:

672
01:04:14.375 --> 01:04:33.917
Data classes and a catalog from day one; field-level encryption with per-customer and legal-retention keys (crypto-shredding for erasure); payments via hosted pages and tokens (no card data); structured logging allow-lists plus PII scanning of sinks; residency and purpose rules as code over a

673
01:04:34.197 --> 01:04:47.914
data-flow manifest; in-country DR; retention schedule enforced by lifecycle; hash-chained audit log anchored externally; consent recorded as data; breach runbook; evidence collected automatically.

674
01:04:49.264 --> 01:04:50.459
Question 16.

675
01:04:50.739 --> 01:04:54.024
How do multi-tenancy and data residency interact?

676
01:04:54.304 --> 01:04:55.669
Thirty-second answer:

677
01:04:55.949 --> 01:05:00.472
Tenants (or markets) may have different residency and contractual requirements.

678
01:05:00.752 --> 01:05:17.989
Use regional cells or silos for tenants whose data must stay in a jurisdiction, pool the rest, route by tenant to the right region, keep per-tenant keys for crypto-shredding and contract termination, and make residency a property in the tenant registry that policy checks consult.

679
01:05:19.339 --> 01:05:20.683
Question 17.

680
01:05:20.963 --> 01:05:25.230
How do you keep compliance mechanisms from eroding as the system evolves?

681
01:05:25.510 --> 01:05:26.875
Thirty-second answer:

682
01:05:27.155 --> 01:05:30.675
The same way you keep architecture from eroding: automation.

683
01:05:30.955 --> 01:05:50.454
Policy checks in CI on every manifest and infrastructure change, PII scanners on every sink, audit-log verification jobs, catalog coverage metrics, drift detection between documented and actual data flows, periodic access reviews, and privacy review built into the design-doc template.

684
01:05:51.804 --> 01:05:52.977
Question 18.

685
01:05:53.257 --> 01:05:57.951
Hash chain versus append-only database versus blockchain for audit logs?

686
01:05:58.231 --> 01:05:59.596
Thirty-second answer:

687
01:05:59.876 --> 01:06:06.489
An append-only table (permissions preventing UPDATE or DELETE) stops casual edits but not a DBA.

688
01:06:06.769 --> 01:06:12.209
A hash chain makes any edit detectable but can be rewritten by whoever controls the whole store.

689
01:06:12.489 --> 01:06:18.719
Periodic external anchoring to a write-once store controlled by someone else fixes that at low cost.

690
01:06:18.999 --> 01:06:30.903
A blockchain (distributed consensus among mutually distrusting parties) is rarely needed inside one organisation, anchoring gives the same tamper evidence with far less complexity.

691
01:06:31.183 --> 01:06:35.193
Managed immutable ledgers and WORM storage are practical options.

692
01:06:36.543 --> 01:06:37.781
Question 19.

693
01:06:38.061 --> 01:06:40.749
How do you balance compliance with product velocity?

694
01:06:41.029 --> 01:06:42.394
Thirty-second answer:

695
01:06:42.674 --> 01:07:01.021
Make the compliant path the easy path: a logging library that only emits allow-listed fields, an encryption library that handles keys, templates for new services with classification and retention defaults, policy checks with clear messages, and a lightweight privacy review for new data uses.

696
01:07:01.301 --> 01:07:08.127
Concentrate heavy process on high-risk data (payments, children's data, sensitive inferences) and new purposes.

697
01:07:09.477 --> 01:07:10.587
Question 20.

698
01:07:10.867 --> 01:07:14.429
What distinguishes a principal engineer's approach to compliance?

699
01:07:14.709 --> 01:07:16.075
Thirty-second answer:

700
01:07:16.355 --> 01:07:33.251
They treat regulations as design inputs from day one, translate them into a small set of mechanisms that make violations structurally hard (encryption with destroyable keys, residency by policy, allow-listed logging, tamper-evident audit), automate the evidence,

701
01:07:33.531 --> 01:07:45.691
know the limits of each mechanism (L2's residual false positives, L4's unanchored window), and work with legal as partners, asking precise questions rather than guessing at the law.

702
01:07:47.041 --> 01:07:47.937
Part 9.

703
01:07:48.217 --> 01:07:49.625
Summary and Practice.

704
01:07:50.875 --> 01:07:51.963
Key takeaways.

705
01:07:52.243 --> 01:08:02.291
Compliance obligations are NFRs with legal consequences; translate each into a testable mechanism and automate the evidence (§2.1).

706
01:08:02.571 --> 01:08:10.123
Know your data: classes, inventory, owners, purposes, retention, everything else depends on it (§2.3).

707
01:08:10.403 --> 01:08:20.024
Minimise and encrypt: pass IDs not PII, pseudonymise analytics, encrypt personal fields with keys you can destroy (§2.4).

708
01:08:20.304 --> 01:08:28.731
Erasure must cover every copy; crypto-shredding makes it one provable action while legal retention uses a separate key (L1).

709
01:08:30.081 --> 01:08:37.057
Find stray PII with validated, structure-aware scanning, and fix it upstream with logging allow-lists (L2).

710
01:08:37.337 --> 01:08:46.126
Encode residency, transfer and purpose rules as code over the architecture's data flows; DR and vendors are data flows (L3).

711
01:08:46.406 --> 01:08:52.955
Keep card data out with hosted pages and tokens; keep payment data in-country (§2.5).

712
01:08:53.235 --> 01:08:59.763
Make audit trails tamper-evident with hash chains and external anchors, and know the unanchored window (L4).

713
01:09:01.113 --> 01:09:12.079
Be breach-ready and proportionate: inventory, logs, runbooks, drills, and effort concentrated on the riskiest data (§2.9, §2.10).

714
01:09:13.429 --> 01:09:14.751
Decision cheat sheet.

715
01:09:15.031 --> 01:09:16.375
Here is a quick table:

716
01:09:16.655 --> 01:09:18.149
Need, and Mechanism.

717
01:09:18.429 --> 01:09:20.733
Erasure across all stores and backups:

718
01:09:21.013 --> 01:09:24.405
Per-subject keys + crypto-shredding + erasure log.

719
01:09:24.685 --> 01:09:26.455
Keep records the law requires:

720
01:09:26.735 --> 01:09:30.703
Legal-retention class, separate key, WORM, retention schedule.

721
01:09:30.983 --> 01:09:32.391
No PII in logs:

722
01:09:32.671 --> 01:09:36.447
Structured logging allow-lists + key-aware scanner + redaction.

723
01:09:36.727 --> 01:09:38.242
Payment data in-country:

724
01:09:38.522 --> 01:09:42.085
Residency policy-as-code; in-country DR and backups.

725
01:09:42.365 --> 01:09:43.517
No card data:

726
01:09:43.797 --> 01:09:48.618
Hosted payment pages, network tokens, isolated vault if unavoidable.

727
01:09:48.898 --> 01:09:50.477
New use of personal data:

728
01:09:50.757 --> 01:09:54.725
Purpose check against consent or legitimate use; privacy review.

729
01:09:55.005 --> 01:09:56.669
Analytics on personal data:

730
01:09:56.949 --> 01:09:59.402
Pseudonymised or aggregated datasets.

731
01:10:00.752 --> 01:10:02.160
Trustworthy audit trail:

732
01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:06.707
Hash chain + frequent external WORM anchors + separation of duties.

733
01:10:06.987 --> 01:10:08.821
Breach notification in hours:

734
01:10:09.101 --> 01:10:11.405
Inventory, logs, runbook, drills.

735
01:10:12.755 --> 01:10:13.886
Exercises.

736
01:10:14.166 --> 01:10:20.907
(B2) Extend L1 with a Kafka topic store holding OrderPlaced events with encrypted customer fields.

737
01:10:21.187 --> 01:10:24.707
Show that crypto-shredding covers the topic's retained history.

738
01:10:24.987 --> 01:10:31.601
(B2) Add detectors to L2 for UPI IDs and GSTINs (GSTIN has a checksum character).

739
01:10:31.881 --> 01:10:34.227
Measure precision with and without the checksum.

740
01:10:34.507 --> 01:10:42.145
(B3) Extend L3's policy with a children's data class that may not flow to recommendations or marketing systems.

741
01:10:42.425 --> 01:10:44.046
Add a flow that violates it.

742
01:10:45.396 --> 01:10:53.353
(B3) In L4, shard the audit log into 8 chains (per service) and anchor a Merkle root of the 8 chain heads each minute.

743
01:10:53.633 --> 01:10:55.404
How does verification change?

744
01:10:55.684 --> 01:11:03.172
(B4) Write the data inventory for your own system: classes, stores, purposes, retention, locations, processors.

745
01:11:03.452 --> 01:11:05.799
Which flows would L3-style rules block?

746
01:11:07.149 --> 01:11:08.151
Further reading.

747
01:11:08.431 --> 01:11:19.823
The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and the DPDP Rules, 2025 (MeitY), primary texts; check current commencement status.

748
01:11:20.103 --> 01:11:29.127
RBI, Storage of Payment System Data (2018) and its FAQs; RBI directions on card-on-file tokenisation.

749
01:11:29.407 --> 01:11:34.399
PCI Security Standards Council, PCI DSS v4.x.

750
01:11:34.679 --> 01:11:41.122
CERT-In, Directions under section 70B(6) of the IT Act (April 2022).

751
01:11:41.402 --> 01:11:50.490
GDPR (Regulation (EU) 2016 or 679) and EDPB guidance on pseudonymisation and transfers.

752
01:11:51.840 --> 01:11:54.144
Ann Cavoukian, Privacy by Design:

753
01:11:54.424 --> 01:11:56.344
The 7 Foundational Principles.

754
01:11:56.624 --> 01:12:03.429
NIST Privacy Framework; ISO or IEC 27001 and 27701.

755
